  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Three schoolgirls started laughing and couldn't stop for days. Kashasha, Tanganyika, the thirtieth of January, nineteen sixty-two.",
        "visual_query": "tanzanian village schoolyard",
        "visual_fallback": "rural classroom"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It spread. Ninety-five of the school's one hundred fifty-nine pupils were affected, in fits that ran from hours to sixteen days.",
        "visual_query": "east african mission school",
        "visual_fallback": "school corridor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Doctors found nothing. No fever, no toxin, no poisoned food. So what travels through a school and leaves no trace in the blood?",
        "visual_query": "1960s african clinic examination",
        "visual_fallback": "doctor notes"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The school closed in March. The laughter went home with the girls, reached Nshamba village, and eventually touched about a thousand people across fourteen schools.",
        "visual_query": "lake victoria western shore village",
        "visual_fallback": "lakeside village"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Medical officers Rankin and Philip published the answer in nineteen sixty-three: mass psychogenic illness. Real symptoms, no pathogen, stress moving through a community like weather.",
        "visual_query": "1960s medical journal archive",
        "visual_fallback": "old medical papers"
      }
    ],
